  "title": "Leviticus: holiness and sacrifice",
  "byline": "Study 47 · God and Christian Faith · First content draft · 2026-11-06",
  "close": {
   "title": "Read Leviticus again",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "runs": [
      "Leviticus closes its promises with God among His people: “I will walk among you and be your God, and you will be My people” (",
      {
       "r": "LEV.26.12",
       "t": "26:12"
      },
      " · BSB). Paul gives that promise to a church: “For we are the temple of the living God. As God has said: ‘I will dwell with them and walk among them, and I will be their God, and they will be My people’” (",
      {
       "r": "2CO.6.16",
       "t": "2 Corinthians 6:16"
      },
      " · BSB). The book that seems farthest from Christian life describes it: a holy God, a sacrifice that opens the way, a people set apart to love Him and their neighbour."
     ]
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "runs": [
      "Read the passage again before you answer."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "question": [
    "God says to you, through Leviticus and through Peter, “Be holy, because I am holy.” Where this week, in a field, a courtroom or a neighbour’s house, is He asking you to live as someone He has already set apart?"
   ]
  }
